@book
@title Litweb

Litweb keeps a program and the explanation of that program in one readable
source. Here is a complete small example:

````text
@code_type rust .rs
@title Distance

@s Squared Distance
The formula $d^2 = x^2 + y^2$ gives the squared distance.
The implementation is in @{distance.rs}.

--- distance.rs
fn squared_distance(x: f64, y: f64) -> f64 {
    x * x + y * y
}
---
````

Tangling follows the root named `distance.rs` and writes the ordinary Rust
file:

```rust
fn squared_distance(x: f64, y: f64) -> f64 {
    x * x + y * y
}
```

Weaving makes a reading copy that keeps the explanation and code together.
The default command,

```text
lw --weave distance.lit
```

writes an offline HTML page. It numbers the section, typesets the equation in
the browser, and links `⟨distance.rs 1⟩` to the displayed code definition.
Selecting the other woven format,

```text
lw --weave --format latex distance.lit
```

writes a LaTeX document and Litweb's packaged style. Compiling that document
with LuaLaTeX produces a PDF with the same section, equation, definition, and
link. HTML and LaTeX look different because they use different output syntax,
but they preserve the same authored meaning.

For Rust books, the PDF can also place a small identifier reminder below a
right-hand page. It lists definitions used on the facing two-page spread when
those definitions are elsewhere, so a reader can follow the code without
repeatedly turning to the final index. The LaTeX chapter explains how these
mini-indexes remain attached to the physical pages where their uses appear.

This book is itself built that way. Its `.lit` chapters are the canonical
production source, not commentary copied beside the implementation. Named code
fragments let the book explain an idea where a reader needs it and later
assemble those fragments into the committed Rust modules under `src/`.

The woven notation makes that assembly visible. `⟨Name 4⟩ ≡` defines a named
fragment in presentation section 4, `+≡` adds to it, and `:=` replaces its
current contents. A bare `⟨Name 4⟩` inside code inserts that fragment. Bold
names are roots: complete files rather than supporting fragments. The linked
numbers are reading locations, not source line numbers.

The implementation follows one pipeline. Parser records the prose and code
occurrences in one source file. Book loads the chapters named in a manifest.
Resolver decides which occurrences define, add to, replace, or use the same
named block. Tangler follows whole-line references from output roots to plan
ordinary files. Prose recognizes paragraphs, lists, examples, tables,
equations, and inline forms, while Identifier locates useful Rust names and
conservatively associates uses with reliable declaration meanings.

Woven Document then prepares the numbered sections, relationships, and
identifier locations that every reading copy must preserve, including the
marker assignments needed by a physical-page index. Weaver renders the shared
facts as HTML; Book Weaving and Presentation extend the HTML path with multiple
pages, navigation, offline equations, and syntax highlighting. LaTeX renders
the same facts as one typeset document and composes Rust mini-indexes on its
page spreads. Output checks and writes all planned files, and Main connects the
library stages to the `lw` command.

Read the chapters in that order. Each chapter asks one main question: what did
the previous stage produce, what new decision is needed, and what exact value
will the following stage receive?

